.TH WS-GEOLOC 1 "September 2026" "ws-node 1.4.9" "WildlifeSystems Commands"
.SH NAME
ws-geoloc \- view and edit the static geolocation of a WildlifeSystems node
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ws-geoloc
.RB [ \-\-file
.IR path ]
.br
.B ws-geoloc
.RB [ \-\-lat
.IR deg ]
.RB [ \-\-lon
.IR deg ]
.RB [ \-\-alt
.IR m ]
.RB [ \-\-acc
.IR m ]
.RB [ \-\-file
.IR path ]
.br
.B ws-geoloc \-\-show
.RB [ \-\-file
.IR path ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ws-geoloc
views and edits
.IR /etc/geolocation ,
the GeoClue static location source. This gives a node a fixed position when no
other location source (GPS, WiFi, or modem) is available, which is the usual
case for a deployed WildlifeSystems node.
.PP
The file holds four newline\-separated floats: latitude, longitude, altitude in
metres, and an accuracy radius in metres. See
.BR geoclue (5),
section
.BR [static\-source] .
.PP
Without arguments the command prompts for each value in turn. Current values,
if any, are offered as defaults and kept by pressing Enter. Each entry is
checked for being a number and for being in range: latitude \-90 to 90,
longitude \-180 to 180, altitude \-500 to 9000, accuracy 1 to 1000000. Accuracy
is a confidence radius, so an unknown position is expressed as a large number:
roughly 100 m for a house, 5000 m a village, 25000 m a city, 100000 m a region.
.PP
Given any of
.BR \-\-lat ,
.BR \-\-lon ,
.BR \-\-alt ,
or
.BR \-\-acc ,
the command does not prompt and does not ask for confirmation: it writes the
values given and exits. This is the form to use from a script, a provisioning
run, or over a non\-interactive
.BR ssh (1)
connection. Values that are not given keep whatever is already in the file, so
.B ws-geoloc \-\-lon 5
changes the longitude alone. Where the file has no usable value, altitude
defaults to 0 and accuracy to 1000 m, but latitude and longitude have no
default: setting a location for the first time needs both
.B \-\-lat
and
.BR \-\-lon .
A value that is not a number or is out of range is rejected with exit status
10 before anything is written.
.PP
Nothing is written until the summary is confirmed, except in the
non\-interactive form above, where the summary is printed and the write
proceeds. An existing file is copied to a timestamped
.I .bak
file first, and the new contents are written to a temporary file in the same
directory and renamed into place, so the geolocation file is never left
half\-written. Root is required to write it;
.B ws-geoloc
re\-executes itself under
.BR sudo (8)
if it is not already running as root.
.PP
The file is inert unless the static source is enabled in
.IR /etc/geoclue/geoclue.conf .
If it is not,
.B ws-geoloc
says so after writing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-lat " deg"
Set the latitude, \-90 to 90, positive north. May also be spelled
.BR \-\-latitude .
.TP
.BI \-\-lon " deg"
Set the longitude, \-180 to 180, positive east. May also be spelled
.BR \-\-longitude .
.TP
.BI \-\-alt " m"
Set the altitude in metres, \-500 to 9000. Defaults to 0 when the file holds no
usable value. May also be spelled
.BR \-\-altitude .
.TP
.BI \-\-acc " m"
Set the accuracy radius in metres, 1 to 1000000. Defaults to 1000 when the file
holds no usable value. May also be spelled
.BR \-\-accuracy .
.PP
Each of the four takes its value as a separate argument
.RB ( "\-\-lat 4" )
or after an equals sign
.RB ( \-\-lat=4 ).
Any of them switches off prompting, and none may be combined with
.BR \-\-show .
.TP
.B \-\-show
Print the current values and exit. Does not prompt.
.TP
.BI \-\-file " path"
Operate on
.I path
instead of
.IR /etc/geolocation .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/geolocation
The GeoClue static source: latitude, longitude, altitude, accuracy radius.
.TP
.I /etc/geoclue/geoclue.conf
GeoClue configuration. The
.B [static\-source]
section must contain
.B enable=true
for the geolocation file to be used.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GEOLOC_FILE
Overrides the default geolocation file path.
.TP
.B GEOCLUE_CONF
Overrides the default GeoClue configuration path.
.TP
.B GEOLOC_NO_ROOT
When set to 1, skips the root check and the re\-execution under
.BR sudo (8).
Intended for testing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 1
Error
.TP
.B 3
The geolocation file is missing or malformed
.TP
.B 10
Invalid argument
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B sudo ws-geoloc \-\-lat 51.4967 \-\-lon \-0.1764
Set latitude and longitude, keeping the existing altitude and accuracy.
.TP
.B sudo ws-geoloc \-\-lon 5
Change the longitude and leave everything else as it is.
.TP
.B sudo ws-geoloc \-\-lat 4 \-\-lon \-4 \-\-alt 12 \-\-acc 100
Set all four values in one go.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR geoclue (5),
.BR ws-heartbeat (1),
.BR ws-indicate (1)
